title: Compliance Frameworks in Cloud
page: compliance-frameworks.html
as-of: 2026-11-25
promise: Frameworks overlap heavily, so run the one with revenue or regulatory force attached as your primary and layer overlays for everything else.

== The 30-second version
layout: text
section: top
Frameworks are external requirements you map your internal control set to, and they overlap heavily. Run the one with revenue or regulatory force attached as your primary; layer overlays for the rest. Compliance is not security: build for both.
notes:
Compliance frameworks are external requirements that you map your internal control set to. SOC 2, ISO 27001 and most other security frameworks share the bulk of their underlying controls, which means a second framework is far cheaper than the first if you build the mapping deliberately.

The page's advice is to pick the framework with revenue or regulatory force attached, run it as your primary, and layer overlays for everything else.

The page adds one caution: compliance is not security. The auditor's sign-off and the attacker's failure are independent events. Build for both.

== Which frameworks apply to you?
layout: table
section: decision-tree
row: If you | Expect
row: Sell B2B SaaS to North American enterprises | SOC 2 Type II, the default ask
row: Sell to EU enterprises, or operate outside North America | ISO 27001, more commonly preferred
row: Store, process or transmit payment card data | PCI DSS v4.0.1, non-negotiable
row: Touch PHI in the US | HIPAA, almost always backed by HITRUST CSF
row: Work in a regulated industry | Sector overlays such as FERPA, GLBA, CJIS, TISAX
The answer is the intersection of what customers require to buy, what regulators require to operate, and the contracts you've signed.
notes:
The page calls this the meta-question every practitioner asks first. The answer is the intersection of three forces: what your customers require to buy, what regulators require to operate, and what contracts you've already signed.

For B2B SaaS selling to North American enterprises, SOC 2 Type II is the default ask; without one, deals stall in security review. If you sell to EU enterprises, or operate outside North America, ISO 27001 is more commonly preferred, often co-required with SOC 2 above about $50k ACV, and ISO 27017 for cloud and ISO 27018 for PII in cloud are often bundled.

If you store, process or transmit payment card data, PCI DSS v4.0.1 is non-negotiable: scope-reduce aggressively via tokenization before you map controls. If you touch Protected Health Information in the US, it is HIPAA, almost always backed by a HITRUST CSF assessment that healthcare customers accept as the unified audit deliverable.

Regulated industries add sector-specific frameworks: FERPA for education, the GLBA Safeguards Rule for financial, CJIS for criminal-justice data, TISAX for automotive, ISO 13485 for medical-device QMS and NERC CIP for North American electric utilities.

== Government and regulatory triggers
layout: table
section: decision-tree
row: If you | Expect
row: Sell to the US federal government | FedRAMP
row: Handle FCI or CUI as a DoD contractor | CMMC 2.0 Level 1, 2 or 3
row: Are a US public company or its subsidiary | SOX ITGCs
row: Process EU or UK residents' personal data | GDPR and UK GDPR
row: Process Californians' data, past a CCPA threshold | CCPA / CPRA
row: Run EU essential or important services | NIS2; DORA for financial services
Most companies eventually accumulate 3-5 frameworks, almost always in this order: revenue gate, deal-type gate, regulatory threshold, sector overlay. Plan the sequence.
notes:
Selling to the US federal government means FedRAMP at Low, Moderate, High or LI-SaaS, plus FISMA for the agency itself; state and local governments often accept GovRAMP, formerly StateRAMP, or a TX-RAMP equivalent. A DoD contractor handling Federal Contract Information or Controlled Unclassified Information needs CMMC 2.0 Level 1, 2 or 3 depending on data sensitivity, plus the DFARS 252.204-7012 contractual requirements. A US public company, or a subsidiary of one, needs SOX ITGCs over its financial-reporting systems.

GDPR and UK GDPR apply to personal data of EU and UK residents regardless of where your company is incorporated. CCPA and CPRA apply to California residents' data once any one of the CCPA's thresholds is met, on revenue or on buying, selling or sharing personal information. A growing number of US states have parallel laws, and most converge on similar primitives. NIS2 covers EU operators of essential and important services, and for financial services DORA covers operational resilience, applying since January 2025.

Most companies eventually accumulate 3-5 frameworks. The order is almost always the framework that gates revenue, usually SOC 2 or PCI DSS; then the one that gates a specific deal type, such as HIPAA, FedRAMP or ISO 27001; then the regulatory framework that becomes mandatory at a threshold, such as GDPR, SOX or CCPA; then the sector-specific overlay. Plan the sequence; don't pursue them ad hoc.

== One control, many names
layout: table
section: overlap
row: Framework | Where MFA on privileged accounts lives
row: SOC 2 | CC6.1
row: ISO 27001 | Spread across the Annex A authentication controls
row: PCI DSS | Requirement 8.4
row: NIST 800-53 | IA-2(1)
The engineering work is the same. Maintain one internal control set, map every external framework to it, and add framework-specific overlays only where required.
notes:
SOC 2, ISO 27001, PCI DSS, HIPAA, FedRAMP, NIST CSF, CIS Controls and most other security frameworks all require the same foundational controls; they just describe them differently. Multi-factor authentication on privileged accounts is the same engineering work whether SOC 2 calls it CC6.1, ISO 27001 spreads it across its Annex A authentication controls, PCI DSS calls it Requirement 8.4, or NIST 800-53 calls it IA-2(1). The auditor needs to see the control in the format their framework expects; the underlying control runs once.

The page calls building one control set per framework the most expensive mistake any GRC program can make.

The remainder is where frameworks diverge, and these are the overlays. PCI DSS has hard requirements for network segmentation, cardholder data flow diagrams and quarterly ASV scans that other frameworks don't share. FedRAMP requires US-citizen-only personnel for some baselines. HIPAA requires Breach Notification Rule processes with specific timelines. GDPR requires data subject rights workflows. Build the overlays once, and don't pretend the underlying engineering work is different.

== SOC 2: an attestation, Type I or Type II
layout: compare
section: soc2
col: Type I | Point-in-time: are controls designed appropriately? | Typically 4-8 weeks of fieldwork | Useful as a stepping stone
col: Type II | Period-of-time, usually 6 or 12 months | Did controls operate effectively across the window? | The procurement-acceptable deliverable
The AICPA authors it and licensed CPA firms perform it. SOC means System and Organization Controls, not a detection team's security operations center.
notes:
SOC 2 is the de facto B2B SaaS attestation in North America. SOC stands for System and Organization Controls; this is not the security operations center of a detection team. The AICPA authors it and licensed CPA firms perform it. It is an attestation report, not a certification: the auditor's opinion on the design or the operating effectiveness of your controls against the Trust Services Criteria.

Type I is point-in-time. The auditor evaluates whether controls are designed appropriately on a specific date, typically with 4-8 weeks of fieldwork. It is useful as a stepping stone, but enterprise buyers usually want Type II.

Type II covers a period of time, usually 6 or 12 months, and the auditor tests whether controls operated effectively across the observation window. That is the procurement-acceptable deliverable.

On choosing a firm, the page says to pick by the lead auditor's cloud literacy and prior engagements on your stack, not the brand on the report, and that stable multi-year relationships drive cost down materially.

== SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria
layout: table
section: soc2
row: Criterion | Status | Notes
row: Security | Required | The bulk of the control universe, CC1-CC9
row: Availability | Optional | Infrastructure-heavy SaaS
row: Confidentiality | Optional | B2B with customer-data sensitivity
row: Processing Integrity | Optional | Fintech and data-processing platforms
row: Privacy | Optional | Less common; GDPR and CCPA already drive parallel work
For a 20-50-person SaaS on a compliance-automation platform: $40k-$80k for a Security-only Type II, $60k-$120k+ adding Availability and Confidentiality.
notes:
Security is required. It is sometimes called the Common Criteria, and the bulk of the control universe lives there, CC1 through CC9: control environment, communication, risk assessment, monitoring, control activities, logical access, system operations, change management and risk mitigation. The other four are optional. Availability covers uptime commitments, capacity planning, environmental protections and DR. Confidentiality covers identifying and protecting confidential data per agreement. Processing Integrity means data is processed completely, accurately, timely and authorized. Privacy is less common because GDPR and CCPA already drive parallel work.

Adding criteria raises the cost. For a 20-50-person SaaS using a compliance-automation platform, the page puts a Type I at $20k-$40k, a Security-only Type II at $40k-$80k, and $60k-$120k+ if you add Availability and Confidentiality. Add 6 months of observation before the audit can be performed: 9-12 months from "we should do this" to a signed Type II report is realistic, 6 months aggressive.

Two cloud-specific gotchas. Almost every SaaS carves out AWS, Azure or GCP as subservice organizations, so you must document the complementary subservice organization controls you assume the provider performs, and the customer must read both reports together. And vendor management is a top finding: auditors check subprocessor lists, signed DPAs and reviewed security questionnaires.

== ISO 27001: a certified management system
layout: timeline
section: iso27001
event: Stage 1 | Documentation review: scope, SoA, risk register, policies
event: Stage 2 | Operational audit; the certification decision is made here
event: Year 1 and Year 2 | Reduced-scope surveillance audits
event: Year 3 | Full recertification audit, then the cycle repeats
Unlike SOC 2, it is a true certification from an accredited registrar. Clauses 4-10 define the mandatory ISMS; Annex A (2022) lists 93 reference controls, and you select the applicable ones in a Statement of Applicability.
notes:
ISO 27001 is the international standard for an Information Security Management System. Unlike SOC 2, it is a true certification, issued by an accredited registrar, and the standard prescribes the management system structure, risk-based with management review, internal audit and continual improvement, in addition to the controls. It is more common than SOC 2 outside North America and increasingly co-required.

Clauses 4-10 define the mandatory ISMS; skipping any of them is a non-conformity that blocks certification. Annex A in the 2022 revision lists 93 reference controls in four themes: Organizational 37, People 8, Physical 14 and Technological 34. You select the applicable ones in a Statement of Applicability, and excluded controls must be justified: the page calls lazy SoAs a top finding. Management review needs evidence of top-management engagement, so programs run by security teams in isolation fail that clause.

ISO 27017 adds cloud-specific controls, 27018 covers PII in public clouds, and 27701 is the privacy extension that maps to GDPR. The page puts initial certification at $25k-$60k for the audit, plus 6-12 months of internal work, with surveillance audits typically 40-60% of the initial cost. The transition window for 2013-revision certificates closed in October 2025, so a current certificate is against the 2022 revision.

== PCI DSS: shrink the scope first
layout: table
section: pci
row: Mechanism | What it does
row: Tokenization | Only the vault holding the PAN stays in scope
row: Hosted payment page or redirect | Your app never touches PAN; SAQ A becomes available
row: Network segmentation | Separates the CDE; misimplemented, it is a top QSA finding
row: Card brand volume thresholds | Level 1 needs an annual QSA-attested ROC; Levels 2-4 can use an SAQ
Anything you can remove from the Cardholder Data Environment saves audit work for the life of the program. v4.0.1 is current; v3.2.1 was fully retired in March 2024.
notes:
PCI DSS is maintained by the PCI Security Standards Council on behalf of the major card brands. It is required if you store, process or transmit cardholder data, or if you can impact the security of cardholder data.

The page says scope reduction has the biggest payoff for the effort. With tokenization, only the tokenization vault holds the PAN: the vault is in scope, everything downstream is not. With a hosted payment page from a PCI Level 1 service provider such as Stripe, Braintree, Adyen or Worldpay, your application never touches PAN, and SAQ A, the lightest of all SAQs, becomes available. Segmentation has to cleanly separate the CDE, with controls verifying it works. Level 1, at 6M or more Visa or Mastercard transactions a year, needs an annual Report on Compliance attested by a Qualified Security Assessor.

The v4.0 changes that matter: a Customized Approach justified by a Targeted Risk Analysis, which the page calls real flexibility for cloud-native architectures; frequency-based verification for many controls rather than annual sampling; MFA for all access into the CDE; and stronger requirements for service providers. AWS, Azure and GCP each publish PCI DSS responsibility matrices. Use the matrix from your provider, and do not let your QSA assume.

== HIPAA: three rules and the BAA
layout: compare
section: hipaa
col: Privacy Rule | Use and disclosure of PHI | Minimum necessary; individual rights
col: Security Rule | Required safeguards for ePHI | Administrative, physical and technical
col: Breach Notification Rule | 60-day notice to affected individuals | 60-day notice to HHS; annual roll-up under 500 | Media notice over 500 in a state
Any subprocessor handling PHI needs a signed Business Associate Agreement (BAA), but it does not automatically cover every service the provider offers. PHI in a non-eligible service is a violation regardless of the BAA.
notes:
HIPAA, from 1996, and the HITECH Act, from 2009, together form the US federal framework for protecting health information. They apply to covered entities, meaning providers, payers and clearinghouses, and to their business associates, including any cloud or SaaS that handles PHI on a covered entity's behalf.

The Security Rule is the technical part for cloud practitioners. HIPAA distinguishes required from addressable implementation specifications. Addressable does not mean optional; it means you implement, or document a justification for an equivalent alternative. The page calls this a common misread.

You must have a signed Business Associate Agreement with any subprocessor that handles PHI, and you must use a HIPAA-eligible service from the provider's list. AWS lists about 150 HIPAA-eligible services and GCP about 140 covered services; Microsoft's BAA covers covered Azure services via the Online Services Terms.

HITRUST CSF is the de facto unified control framework healthcare customers ask for. It harmonizes HIPAA, NIST 800-53, ISO 27001, PCI DSS, COBIT and more into one assessable control set. The r2 assessment is expensive, at $100k-$500k+, but a single r2 satisfies most healthcare customer security reviews and crosswalks to many other frameworks.

== FedRAMP baselines
layout: table
section: fedramp
row: Baseline | Controls | For
row: Low | ~125 | Public-facing or non-sensitive data
row: Moderate | ~325 | CUI and most sensitive-but-unclassified data; ~80% of authorizations
row: High | ~425 | Data whose loss would have severe or catastrophic effect
row: LI-SaaS | ~125, reduced scope | Low-impact SaaS that does not store federal data
Drawn from NIST SP 800-53 Rev 5. An agency-sponsored ATO is the most common path. Moderate through Rev5 takes 12-18+ months and $500k-$2M+ in first-year cost.
notes:
FedRAMP is the standardized authorization process for cloud services used by US federal agencies, drawn from NIST SP 800-53 Rev 5 with a FedRAMP-specific overlay. Moderate is the most common baseline, at about 80% of authorizations. High is required for certain agencies and use cases, such as DoD, IRS and law enforcement.

In the most common path, a federal agency sponsors the authorization and its CIO or CISO issues the Authority to Operate. The JAB P-ATO is legacy: in 2024 the FedRAMP Board replaced the JAB. FedRAMP Ready and In Process are pre-authorization flags, useful for marketing but not equivalent to an ATO. A FedRAMP-accredited 3PAO performs the assessment and writes the SAR and SAP; the package also includes the SSP, a continuous-monitoring strategy and a POA&M for residual findings.

FedRAMP Moderate through Rev5 takes 12-18+ months from kickoff to ATO and $500k-$2M+ in first-year cost, and annual continuous monitoring adds about $300k-$600k. The page's rule is to pursue FedRAMP only with named federal revenue: "Maybe federal someday" is a recipe for sunk cost.

== FedRAMP 20x and the Rev5 cutoff
layout: timeline
section: fedramp
event: April to September 2025 | Low pilot
event: November 2025 to March 2026 | Moderate pilot
event: Now | FedRAMP describes wide-scale adoption; Class A, B and C rules final
event: 11 June 2027 | FedRAMP says it will stop accepting new Rev5 certifications
20x replaces Rev5 with automated continuous monitoring and evidence in place of paperwork. FedRAMP cautions that its future dates may shift. Starting at Low or Moderate? Start with 20x.
notes:
Everything on the previous slide describes FedRAMP's Rev5 process. FedRAMP 20x is its replacement: automated continuous monitoring with key security indicators, evidence in place of paperwork, and a more contemporary control-mapping approach.

It is past the pilot stage. The Low pilot ran from April to September 2025 and the Moderate pilot from November 2025 to March 2026. FedRAMP now describes 20x as in wide-scale adoption, with certification rules finalized for Class A, Class B for Low and Class C for Moderate; a Class D pilot for High is still to come.

The date to plan around is the Rev5 cutoff. FedRAMP says it will stop accepting new Rev5 certifications on 11 June 2027, while cautioning that its future dates are estimates and may shift. A Moderate authorization through Rev5 takes 12-18+ months, so a Rev5 effort started now would likely still be under way when that cutoff arrives. If you are starting FedRAMP at Low or Moderate, the page says to start with 20x and treat Rev5 as the exception that needs a reason.

== CMMC 2.0 levels
layout: figure
section: cmmc
figure: cmmc-levels
Last checked 11 November 2026: Phase II is still suspended, Phase I self-assessments remain, and the 32 CFR final rule was not withdrawn.
notes:
CMMC 2.0 is the US Department of Defense's framework for contractors handling Federal Contract Information or Controlled Unclassified Information. The final rule took effect in December 2024, and phased contract incorporation began in 2025.

Level 1, Foundational, is 17 basic safeguarding practices for FCI, with an annual self-assessment and affirmation by a senior official. Level 2, Advanced, is the 110 controls of NIST SP 800-171 Rev 2 for CUI. A C3PAO assessment every 3 years is the end state the rule describes, but with Phase II suspended, self-assessment with senior-official affirmation still covers a large share of Level 2 contracts. Which applies to you is a question about your specific contract clauses. Level 3, Expert, adds a subset of NIST SP 800-172 and is assessed by the DoD's DIBCAC.

On 13 July 2026 the Department of War, the Department of Defense's secondary title under a September 2025 executive order, announced it was suspending the Phase II requirements, which had been scheduled to take effect on 10 November 2026. As of the page's check on 11 November 2026, the DoD CIO page still describes the suspension and names no restart, and a DFARS class deviation signed on 3 September 2026 carries the suspension into contracting. The 32 CFR final rule was not withdrawn, so this is a pause in phase-in rather than a repeal. Phase II is the step that would have moved most Level 2 contracts to third-party assessment, so do not budget a C3PAO engagement as if that shift had started, and confirm current status on the DoD CIO CMMC page before making a compliance spending decision.

== NIST CSF 2.0: six functions
layout: figure
section: nist-csf
figure: csf-functions
CSF is voluntary and not an attestation. Use it for executive reporting and program structure, layered above your attestation frameworks.
notes:
The NIST Cybersecurity Framework was first published in 2014 and updated to 2.0 in February 2024. It is a voluntary framework, not an attestation, but the page calls it the most-cited common-language reference in security programs worldwide.

The figure shows the six Functions. Govern is the function added in 2.0, covering organizational context, risk management strategy, roles, policies, oversight and cybersecurity supply-chain risk management. The other five are Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond and Recover. Underneath sit 22 Categories and about 106 Subcategories, the actual outcomes you implement, and each Subcategory maps to controls in 800-53, ISO 27001, COBIT, ISA/IEC 62443 and others through the Informative References.

Use CSF as the meta-framework for executive reporting and program structure. Boards and CISOs find its Function and Category vocabulary far more legible than SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria or ISO 27001 Annex A, and many regulators, such as NYDFS and the FTC, reference CSF in expected practice.

== NIST 800-53, 800-171 and 800-172
layout: table
section: nist-800
row: Publication | Scope | Where it shows up
row: SP 800-53 Rev 5 | ~1,000 controls in 20 families | The basis for FedRAMP baselines
row: SP 800-171 Rev 2 | 110 controls in 14 families, a subset of 800-53 | DFARS 252.204-7012 and CMMC 2.0 Level 2
row: SP 800-172 | ~35 enhanced controls for high-value CUI | The CMMC Level 3 control set draws from it
As a private-sector entity you will rarely implement 800-53 directly unless pursuing FedRAMP, but it is the largest control vocabulary: crosswalks to almost everything else flow through it.
notes:
NIST SP 800-53 Rev 5 is the comprehensive US federal control catalog: about 1,000 controls in 20 families, such as Access Control, Audit and Accountability, Identification and Authentication, and System and Communications Protection. Rev 5 put privacy controls inline with security controls and added the supply-chain risk management family.

NIST SP 800-171 protects Controlled Unclassified Information in nonfederal systems and organizations: 110 controls in 14 families, derived as a subset of 800-53 tuned for non-federal contexts. It is the technical baseline behind DFARS 252.204-7012 and CMMC 2.0 Level 2. Rev 3, published in May 2024, restructured the families and updated some controls. The Department of War CIO's CMMC FAQ says the Department will bring Rev 3 in by rulemaking, and that a class deviation keeps assessments on Rev 2 until then.

NIST SP 800-172 layers about 35 enhanced controls on top of 800-171 for environments holding particularly high-value CUI, focused on adversary-targeted attacks such as advanced persistent threats and supply chain.

== CIS Benchmarks and CIS Controls
layout: compare
section: cis
col: CIS Benchmarks | Configuration-level baselines per platform | AWS, Azure and GCP Foundations; Kubernetes; Docker; Ubuntu, Windows, RHEL | What every modern CSPM scores against by default
col: CIS Controls v8.1 | 18 prioritized controls with ~153 safeguards | Three Implementation Groups, by organization profile | Which controls to implement first, by adversary realism
For most cloud-native programs, complying with the CIS Foundations Benchmark is the starting goal most worth the effort.
notes:
The Center for Internet Security publishes two distinct things that are often confused. Benchmarks are specific, configuration-level baselines per platform: CIS AWS, Azure and GCP Foundations, the Kubernetes Benchmark, Docker, Ubuntu, Windows, RHEL and dozens of application and database benchmarks. Rather than attestations, they are the implementation-layer baseline every modern CSPM scores against by default. Aligning with a Foundations Benchmark produces evidence that crosswalks cleanly to SOC 2 CC controls, ISO 27001 Annex A technical controls, PCI DSS Requirements 1, 2, 7, 8 and 10, and HIPAA Security Rule technical safeguards.

The CIS Controls are 18 prioritized security controls with about 153 safeguards, in three Implementation Groups. IG1 is essential cyber hygiene, about 56 safeguards, for small businesses with limited security resources; IG2 adds about 74 for mid-size organizations with some regulatory exposure; IG3 adds about 23 for large enterprises with sensitive data and sophisticated adversaries. The page calls the Controls a useful prioritization layer above your framework of record: they tell you which controls to implement first based on adversary realism, not just compliance order. The California Attorney General's 2016 Data Breach Report called the failure to implement the applicable Controls a lack of reasonable security.

== GDPR and cross-border transfers
layout: table
section: gdpr
row: Transfer mechanism | What it is
row: Adequacy decision | Jurisdictions the Commission found adequate; transfers unrestricted
row: Standard Contractual Clauses | 2021 modular SCCs plus a Transfer Impact Assessment
row: EU-US Data Privacy Framework | US companies self-certify; under ongoing legal challenge
row: Binding Corporate Rules | Intra-group rules; worthwhile only for large multinationals
GDPR applies to EU/EEA residents' personal data wherever you are. Report a breach to the supervisory authority within 72 hours; fines go up to €20M or 4% of global annual turnover.
notes:
GDPR applies to processing of personal data of EU and EEA residents, regardless of where the controller or processor is located, so most cloud services with EU users are in scope. Article 32 requires risk-appropriate technical and organizational measures, including pseudonymization and encryption and regular testing of effectiveness. Data subject rights need workflows for access, rectification, erasure, portability, restriction and objection. The page says enforcement is real, not hypothetical.

The page calls transfers the hard part for cloud workloads: personal data leaving the EEA needs a lawful transfer mechanism. Adequacy decisions cover jurisdictions such as the UK, Switzerland, Japan and Canada for commercial data. The 2021 modular SCCs are the workhorse, paired with a Transfer Impact Assessment documenting that the destination country's laws don't undermine the protections. The EU-US Data Privacy Framework, adopted July 2023, replaced Privacy Shield, which the CJEU invalidated in 2020. The DPF is itself the target of ongoing legal challenge, with Schrems III anticipated, so mature programs use it where applicable but keep SCCs and TIAs as the defensible fallback.

For California and the other US state laws, the page's advice is to operate one privacy program tuned to the strictest standard, typically CPRA or GDPR, and overlay state-specific requirements where required.

== SOX: the four ITGC domains
layout: table
section: sox
row: ITGC domain | What it covers
row: Access to Programs and Data | Logical and privileged access, segregation of duties, access reviews
row: Program Change Management | Change requests, code review, approvals, deployment audit trail
row: Computer Operations | Job scheduling, backup and recovery, incident management
row: Program Development | SDLC controls, testing, separating development from production
Access is the largest source of SOX findings in cloud environments. Build the evidence chain once, with automation, and present it every quarter.
notes:
The Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002 governs US public companies. Section 404 requires management to assess internal controls over financial reporting and the external auditor to attest. The cloud-security-relevant slice is IT General Controls: controls over the IT environment supporting financial reporting systems.

The page names access to programs and data, with user-access reviews on a quarterly cadence, as the largest source of SOX findings in cloud environments.

In the cloud, tying IaC commits to Jira tickets to peer-reviewed pull requests to production deployments produces almost all the program-change evidence. Quarterly access reviews against your identity provider, such as Entra ID, Okta, Google Workspace or AWS IAM Identity Center, cover the access slice. Cloud audit logs such as CloudTrail, Activity Log and Cloud Audit Logs cover the audit-trail requirement. The auditor wants a clean evidence chain: build it once with automation, and present it every quarter.

== EU AI Act: what applies, and when
layout: table
section: ai-governance
row: Obligation | Applies from | Status
row: Prohibited practices; AI literacy | 2 February 2025 | In force
row: GPAI model obligations | 2 August 2025 | In force
row: Transparency duties (Art. 50) | 2 August 2026 | In force; grace on content-marking
row: High-risk, stand-alone (Annex III) | 2 December 2027 | Deferred from 2 August 2026
row: High-risk in regulated products (Annex I) | 2 August 2028 | Deferred from 2 August 2027
The Digital Omnibus on AI deferred the high-risk deadlines. The content-marking grace applies to existing systems. Build the AI system inventory now.
notes:
AI governance is the newest compliance surface. The EU AI Act is the first broad AI law, and it is risk-tiered: prohibited practices are banned outright, high-risk systems need conformity assessment, risk management, logging, human oversight and registration, limited-risk systems carry transparency duties, and minimal-risk systems are unregulated. General-purpose AI models carry their own obligations. Like GDPR, it is extraterritorial: it applies if your AI output is used in the EU.

The original timeline had high-risk obligations landing on 2 August 2026. The Commission's Digital Omnibus on AI, proposed 19 November 2025, deferred the high-risk deadlines and entered into force on 27 July 2026. The heaviest obligations moved out by roughly sixteen months, while the transparency duties arrived on schedule; the grace period on content-marking for existing systems runs to 2 December 2026.

The deferral was granted largely because the harmonised standards that create a presumption of conformity are not published yet. Two things are worth doing now regardless: build the AI system inventory, because you cannot classify what you have not enumerated, and treat the transparency duties as live today. Most member states are behind on designating market-surveillance authorities, so the fact that nobody has come asking is not evidence that your classification is right.

ISO/IEC 42001 is the certifiable management-system standard for AI, analogous to ISO 27001's ISMS. If you already run ISO 27001, the page calls 42001 the lowest-friction way to get credit for AI governance.

== Crosswalks: the mapping is mostly done
layout: table
section: crosswalks
row: Tool | What it gives you
row: Secure Controls Framework | Free; ~1,100 meta-controls mapped to ~150 frameworks
row: Unified Compliance Framework | Commercial; the database many GRC platforms license
row: OSCAL | NIST's machine-readable format for controls, profiles and SSPs
row: CSA Cloud Controls Matrix | Cloud-specific controls, plus the CAIQ questionnaire for STAR
row: NIST CSF Informative References | Official CSF 2.0 mappings to 800-53, ISO 27001 and others
Use these instead of reinventing the crosswalk.
notes:
The page's advice is to use these instead of reinventing the crosswalk. The Secure Controls Framework is free, with about 1,100 meta-controls mapped to about 150 frameworks, maintained by ComplianceForge; the page calls it a free place to start. The Unified Compliance Framework is commercial and exhaustive, the database many GRC platforms license behind the scenes: expensive but comprehensive.

OSCAL is NIST's machine-readable XML, JSON and YAML format for controls, profiles, components and SSPs. FedRAMP authorization packages are migrating to it, and the page says smart programs author internal catalogs natively in OSCAL.

The CSA Cloud Controls Matrix is a cloud-specific control framework, and the CAIQ is the questionnaire vendors complete for the CSA STAR Registry, widely accepted by enterprise procurement. NIST's Informative References give official mappings from CSF 2.0 Subcategories to 800-53, 800-171, ISO 27001, COBIT, ISA 62443 and others. CIS publishes its own crosswalks from CIS Controls v8.1 to other frameworks in the CIS Controls Navigator.

== AWS, Azure and GCP compliance programs
layout: table
section: provider-comparison
row: Capability | AWS | Azure | GCP
row: Total attestations | 140+ | 100+ | 75+
row: Evidence portal | AWS Artifact | Service Trust Portal | Compliance Reports Manager
row: Customer assessment tooling | AWS Audit Manager | Purview Compliance Manager | Assured Workloads + SCC compliance posture
row: Regulated-workload boundary | GovCloud (US), Secret Region | Azure Government, Sovereign Cloud | Assured Workloads, partner sovereign clouds
Attestation coverage at the provider level is roughly parity. The differences that matter are at the customer-implementation layer and in the regulated-boundary offerings.
notes:
Every major cloud attests to dozens of frameworks at the provider layer. This is an excerpt of the page's comparison table, which also covers SOC reports, ISO certifications, PCI DSS, HIPAA, FedRAMP, DoD Impact Levels, CMMC support, EU and APAC regulations, and BAA signing.

The page draws three takeaways. First, attestation coverage at the provider level is roughly parity across all three; the differences that matter are at the customer-implementation layer and in the regulated-boundary offerings. Second, the evidence portals, Artifact, the Service Trust Portal and Reports Manager, are where you collect provider AOCs and SOC reports for your auditor, so bookmark all three early. Third, the customer-side assessment tools, Audit Manager, Purview Compliance Manager, and Assured Workloads with SCC, are valuable, but they don't replace a GRC platform: they instrument the cloud-native portion of evidence collection.

== Continuous evidence, and the platform that holds it
layout: text
section: continuous | platforms
Every modern framework now expects continuous monitoring evidence: show me the rule, the evaluation log, the remediation workflow. When choosing a GRC platform, integration depth with your clouds, identity provider and ticketing matters most.
notes:
Every modern framework now expects continuous monitoring evidence rather than point-in-time snapshots. PCI DSS v4.0, FedRAMP 20x, SOC 2 with revised TSC, and the trend across ISO 27001 audits all push toward "show me the rule, show me the evaluation log, show me the remediation workflow" instead of a screenshot taken last Tuesday. Programs with compliance-as-code in CI/CD, CSPM or CNAPP rule packs aligned to their target frameworks, and a platform that pulls evidence on a schedule sail through audits; programs still on screenshot-driven evidence collection are about to face increasing auditor friction.

The GRC platform is the system-of-record layer that organizes evidence, framework mappings, exceptions, vendor risk and audit readiness. The page describes Vanta, Drata, Secureframe, Hyperproof, OneTrust, ServiceNow GRC, Optro (formerly AuditBoard) and LogicGate, each with a different emphasis. The criterion that matters most is integration depth with your clouds, identity provider and ticketing, because the whole value proposition is automated evidence collection. A platform that pulls 80% of evidence automatically is worth 2-3 times one that pulls 30%. Talk to your target auditor about which platforms they are efficient on before signing the contract.

== Common pitfalls
layout: list
section: pitfalls
item: Chasing every framework
item: Confusing compliance with security
item: Screenshot-driven evidence collection
item: Treating PCI DSS scope as unavoidable
item: Putting PHI in services the BAA does not cover
item: FedRAMP without revenue
item: Conflating data residency with data sovereignty
The page lists three more: no continuous monitoring, ignoring vendor and subprocessor risk, and letting framework revisions sneak up.
notes:
Five frameworks is a real program; ten is a paperwork factory. Pursue frameworks against named revenue or regulation, and defer the rest. The auditor will sign off whether the breach is coming or not, so risk-rank, then implement, and do not let SOC 2 priorities crowd out the controls that close real attack paths. The page notes that the Capital One breach was at a SOC-compliant company.

Past 20 controls, screenshot-driven evidence dominates program cost and goes stale immediately; automate collection from cloud APIs, the IdP, the code repo and MDM. Tokenization and hosted payment pages collapse your CDE to almost nothing. Using a non-eligible service with PHI is a HIPAA violation even if the BAA is signed, so check the provider's HIPAA-eligible services list every release. "Maybe federal someday" has burned 18-month, $1M+ programs that never reached an agency contract: get the sponsor first. A region in the right country doesn't, by itself, address regulator concerns about cloud-provider personnel access, foreign legal subpoena or sovereign-cloud requirements, so read the specific regulation and check what each candidate offering guarantees on each point.

The other three: frameworks increasingly expect continuous control verification, not annual sampling; vendor and subprocessor risk is a top finding across SOC 2, ISO 27001 and GDPR audits; and ISO 27001:2022, PCI DSS v4.0, NIST CSF 2.0 and NIST 800-171 Rev 3 are all real transitions with deadlines. Mapping debt is silent until it isn't.

== Which framework should I start with?
layout: question
section: faq
Consider who is asking for a framework, and what it unlocks for the business.
notes:
This is a question from the page's FAQ. Ask it and let the room answer before moving on.

== The one your customers ask for
layout: answer
section: faq
Pick the one with revenue attached: for most B2B SaaS in North America, SOC 2 Type II. Run it as your primary and map every other framework to it.
notes:
Start with the one your customers ask for. For most B2B SaaS in North America that is SOC 2 Type II. If you sell to EU enterprises, ISO 27001 is the more common ask and may be required co-equally. If you handle payment cards, PCI DSS is non-negotiable. If you handle PHI, HIPAA. If you sell to the US federal government, FedRAMP.

Pick the one with revenue attached, run it as your primary, and map every other framework to it. They overlap heavily, so a second framework is far cheaper than the first.

== Does using AWS, Azure or GCP make my workload compliant?
layout: question
section: faq
Consider which controls the provider's own reports cover, and which ones your auditor still has to see from you.
notes:
This is the second FAQ question. Again, ask it before showing the answer.

== No: only the provider's part of the stack
layout: answer
section: faq
The provider attests to the controls it owns: physical security, hypervisor, managed-service internals. You attest to yours: IAM configuration, encryption decisions, application security, customer access.
notes:
No: it makes the provider's part of the stack compliant. Under the shared responsibility model, the cloud provider attests to the controls they own, such as physical security, the hypervisor and managed-service internals, and you attest to the controls you own: IAM configuration, data encryption decisions, application security and customer access.

AWS Artifact, the Azure Service Trust Portal and Google Cloud Compliance Reports Manager give you the provider-side reports to share with your auditor; you still need to demonstrate your half. Inheriting a provider's FedRAMP authorization does not authorize your service; the underlying platform is simply in scope of the authorization you will pursue.
